Create an AMI (WordPressAMI)
Once your EC2 instance is fully configured with WordPress:
bash
# Stop Apache to ensure consistency
sudo systemctl stop httpd
Go to EC2 Console → Instances → Actions → Create Image (AMI).
Name it: WordPressAMI.
This AMI will be used in the Launch Template for scaling.
2️⃣ Create a Target Group (TG)
Navigate to EC2 → Target Groups → Create Target Group.
Choose Instances as target type.
Health check:
Protocol: HTTP
Path: /index.php or /readme.html
Register your EC2 instance (or later, ASG instances will auto-register).
3️⃣ Create an Application Load Balancer (ALB)
Go to EC2 → Load Balancers → Create Load Balancer → Application Load Balancer.
Name: WordPress-ALB.
Scheme: Internet-facing.
Select VPC and subnets (at least 2 AZs).
Security Group: Allow HTTP (80).
Listener: HTTP → Forward to Target Group (WordPress-TG).
4️⃣ Create a Launch Template
Navigate to EC2 → Launch Templates → Create Launch Template.
Name: WordPress-LT.
Select AMI: WordPressAMI.
Instance type: t2.micro (or as per requirement).
Security Group: Allow HTTP (80).
User Data (optional): Add startup script if needed.
5️⃣ Create an Auto Scaling Group (ASG)
Go to EC2 → Auto Scaling Groups → Create Auto Scaling Group.
Name: WordPress-ASG.
Select Launch Template: WordPress-LT.
Attach to Target Group: WordPress-TG.
Configure scaling policies:
Min size: 2
Desired capacity: 2
Max size: 5
Enable health checks (EC2 + ELB).
Scaling policy example:
Scale out: Add 1 instance if CPU > 70% for 5 minutes.
Scale in: Remove 1 instance if CPU < 30% for 10 minutes.
6️⃣ Access Application
Use the ALB DNS name (e.g., WordPress-ALB-123456.ap-south-1.elb.amazonaws.com).
It will distribute traffic across EC2 instances in multiple AZs.
ASG ensures scaling up/down automatically.
